<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Controls"
             xmlns:views="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Views"
             xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.ViewModels"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="300" d:DesignHeight="700"
             x:Class="KinesisEdit.Views.MacroInspectorPanelView"
             x:DataType="vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel">
  <!--
    The key inspector's Macro panel (mockup 2i) — "selecting a key edits its macro right here; the
    Macros tab is a library, not the editor".

    THE RAIL IS 300px WIDE WHILE THIS PANEL IS SHOWING, not 268. The handoff states both widths and
    this is the macro-editing variant; the rail sets it off the panel's own WantsWideRail, so this
    file names no width at all.

    THERE IS NO ASSIGN BUTTON. Editing in place means the first recorded keystroke IS the macro —
    the view model creates it and puts it in the key's first free slot. The old panel's
    draft-then-assign pair was a modal's shape.

    NOTHING HERE IS A GLYPH STANDING IN FOR AN ICON. The record dot is an Ellipse, the drag grip is
    `IconDragHandle` and the insert affordance `IconInsert`: U+25CF, U+283F and U+FF0B are in
    neither embedded IBM Plex family and would tofu. The row's `×` (U+00D7) and the middle dots
    (U+00B7) ARE characters — both families carry them.

    MONO IS THE FILE'S VOICE. The step tokens, the modifier codes, the millisecond counts and the
    meter readings are values that exist verbatim in a config file or are counted against one;
    every sentence the app says in its own voice is sans.

    THE ROOT IS A GRID SO THE DRAG HAS SOMEWHERE TO DRAW. The panel itself is still the stack it
    always was; the second child is a Canvas that holds the carried row while a drag is in flight
    (issue #128). A Canvas measures to nothing, so it costs the stack no height, and it is the LAST
    child so the ghost draws over the list rather than under it.
  -->

  <UserControl.Resources>
    <!-- One held modifier, drawn. THREE call sites now — the step row, the ghost that copies it,
         and the chord composer's preview — so it is a resource rather than three copies.

         The tooltip is not decoration: only a RIGHT modifier spells its side, so a bare mark is
         worn by the left one and the generic one alike and the row cannot tell them apart.
         `Description` is where the distinction the file really carries stays reachable.

         UP TO TWO RUNS, IN TWO FACES. The key-symbol family is a 19-codepoint subset with NO Latin
         letters, so the `R` side letter stays in the ordinary mono face and only the mark goes in
         `.keySymbol`; a whole `R⇧` set in that class would fall back to a system font for its `R`.
         `MacroModifierMarks.Split` hands the two out separately for exactly this. -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="MacroModifierMarkRun" DataType="vm:MacroModifierMarks+Mark">
      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                  ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Description}">
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding Side}"
                   Classes="monoValueSmall muted"
                   IsVisible="{Binding HasSide}"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding Symbol}"
                   Classes="keySymbol muted"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
      </StackPanel>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!-- ONE TEMPLATE, TWO CALL SITES: the row in the list and the ghost carried above it. The
         ghost has to be a copy of the row, and a second hand-written copy of six runs in two
         faces is a copy that drifts. Nothing in here binds past the step — the row's commands are
         anchored on the view outside it — so the same template renders correctly in both places. -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="MacroStepCaption" DataType="vm:MacroInspectorStepViewModel">
      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="5" VerticalAlignment="Center">
        <!-- Mono end to end: the number counts the file's own steps and the token is literally
             what layoutN.txt carries. -->
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding NumberText}" Classes="monoValueSmall muted" VerticalAlignment="Center" />

        <!-- THE KEY THAT WAS STRUCK IS TINTED. `MacroStepKeyBrush` is the macro badge's own
             orchid, darkened on light for type — purple already means "macro" in this app, so the
             cap's dot and this token say one thing in one hue, and the row invents no fifth
             meaning. Deliberately not the accent: that has exactly two, and a key token is
             neither. -->
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding TokenText}"
                   Classes="monoValue"
                   Foreground="{DynamicResource MacroStepKeyBrush}"
                   IsVisible="{Binding HasToken}"
                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />

        <!-- The held modifiers as 2i's marks — `⇧`, `R⌃` — and NOT the file's `LS`, `LC`, `S `
             codes, which are what goes on the drive rather than what a person reads
             (`Keystroke.FormatModifiers`, untouched). See `MacroModifierMarkRun` above. -->
        <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Modifiers}"
                      ItemTemplate="{StaticResource MacroModifierMarkRun}"
                      IsVisible="{Binding HasModifiers}"
                      VerticalAlignment="Center">
          <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
            <ItemsPanelTemplate>
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="3" />
            </ItemsPanelTemplate>
          </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
        </ItemsControl>

        <TextBlock Text="{Binding ActionText}" Classes="meta muted" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
      </StackPanel>
    </DataTemplate>
  </UserControl.Resources>

  <UserControl.Styles>
    <!-- ===== The drag, seen ==================================================================
         Two states and no accent between them. The accent has exactly two sanctioned meanings in
         this app — selection/focus, and "you changed this" — and "this row is being carried" is
         neither, so the carry reads as LIFT AND DIM: the source row recedes and a copy of it rides
         above the list on the popover elevation. The drop ring stays the accent's, because that
         one really is about where a selection lands.

         NOTHING HERE ANIMATES, deliberately. The ghost is pinned to the pointer every move; a
         transition on its position would make it lag the very gesture it exists to show, which is
         the complaint this feature answers. Nothing is added to Themes/Motion.axaml because
         nothing moves on a clock. -->
    <Style Selector="Grid.dragSource">
      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.35" />
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="Border.macroStepGhost">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceRaisedBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceBorderRaisedBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
      <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="{DynamicResource ShadowPopover}" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,4" />
    </Style>
  </UserControl.Styles>

  <Grid>
    <StackPanel>

    <!-- ===== The refusal =====================================================================
         Drawn INSTEAD of the editor, never beside it. A device with no macros, or a position that
         can never carry one (05 §5.3, spec 02's verbatim wording), is the design's sanctioned
         exception to "absent features are not shown": the user pressed this tab, and a tab that
         rendered nothing would look broken. Amber, never red — nothing failed. -->
    <Border Classes="inspectorSection" IsVisible="{Binding !IsAvailable}">
      <Border Background="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBrush}"
              BorderBrush="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBorderBrush}"
              BorderThickness="1"
              CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}"
              Padding="9,7">
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding UnavailableReason}"
                   Classes="body statusWarning"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap" />
      </Border>
    </Border>

    <StackPanel IsVisible="{Binding IsAvailable}">

      <!-- ===== The slot ======================================================================
           WHICH OF THE KEY'S MACROS IS BEING EDITED (issue #137). A key holds up to five macros
           told apart by their co-triggers (06 §1); until this strip existed the rail could reach
           only one of them and the Macros tab's slot cards were the only way to the rest.

           ONE DOT PER SLOT THE DIALECT WRITES, never the five the model owns: `PersistedSlotsPerKey`
           is 3 on the Advantage2 and Freestyle Edge/Pro and 5 on the RGB family, and a macro put in
           slot 4 of a Freestyle Edge is gone at the next save.

           NO `ACTIVE` BADGE AND NO `Make active`. The dropdown is a pure EDITING selector — every
           populated slot is live on the keyboard, and what tells them apart is the Trigger strip
           below. Picking a slot writes `KeyboardKey.ActiveMacroIndex`, which is in-memory only and
           never serialized (05 §1.3), so it deliberately does NOT dirty the session.

           ABSENT, NEVER DISABLED: a flat-list board (the Advantage 360) and a position that refuses
           macros (05 §5.3) draw no selector at all. -->
      <Border Classes="inspectorSection" IsVisible="{Binding HasSlotSelector}">
        <StackPanel Spacing="6">
          <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.SlotSectionLabel}" Classes="sectionLabel" />

          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*" ColumnSpacing="8">
            <!-- The dots are geometry and not a glyph, for the record dot's own reason: `●` (U+25CF)
                 is in neither embedded IBM Plex family. Filled means occupied; the caption is the
                 tooltip, because a dot alone is not accessible text. -->
            <ItemsControl Grid.Column="0" ItemsSource="{Binding SlotOptions}" VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                  <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4" />
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
              <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate DataType="vm:MacroSlotOption">
                  <Ellipse Classes="macroSlotDot"
                           Classes.filled="{Binding IsOccupied}"
                           ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Caption}"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                </DataTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            </ItemsControl>

            <ComboBox Grid.Column="1"
                      ItemsSource="{Binding SlotOptions}"
                      SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedSlot, Mode=TwoWay}"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Stretch">
              <ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate DataType="vm:MacroSlotOption">
                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding Caption}" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                </DataTemplate>
              </ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
            </ComboBox>
          </Grid>
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <!-- ===== The trigger ===================================================================
           WHAT THE KEY HAS TO BE PRESSED WITH, and whether anything else on it already claims that
           combination (06 §5). The latches used to sit in the footer under a `CO-TRIGGERS` label,
           three rows away from the slot they distinguish; here they are beside the trigger token
           they qualify and the collision they cause.

           THREE LATCHES, NOT SIX AND NOT FOUR — a deliberate deviation recorded in
           docs/app/design-system.md. spec 10 pins the legacy set at "six co-trigger buttons —
           Left/Right Shift, Ctrl, Alt"; authoring here is left-only, and a right-hand or generic
           co-trigger a FILE carries lights the matching left latch instead of disappearing. There is
           no `⌘` latch: no co-trigger in specs 06 or 10 names one, so it would author a token no
           firmware is documented to honour.

           EACH LATCH IS ONE OR TWO RUNS IN TWO FACES, exactly as the step rows draw them — but the
           left three spell no side, so each is a single `.keySymbol` run today. NEITHER RUN CARRIES
           A COLOUR: `ToggleSegment`'s `.selected` face sets Foreground, a TextBlock inherits it and
           an `Icon` (painting from Stroke) cannot, which is why this site is type at all.

           THE STATUS IS AN ADVISORY AND NEVER A REFUSAL. Amber, never red — a collision is what
           `Validate()` reports as `MacroTriggerCollision`, and it reports rather than blocks. -->
      <Border Classes="inspectorSection">
        <StackPanel Spacing="6">
          <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.TriggerSectionLabel}" Classes="sectionLabel" />

          <!-- A Grid and not a horizontal StackPanel: the token takes the `*` column and trims, so
               nothing here can run off a 300 px rail however long a dialect spells a trigger. -->
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*" ColumnSpacing="6">
            <ItemsControl Grid.Column="0" ItemsSource="{Binding CoTriggers}" IsVisible="{Binding HasCoTriggers}">
              <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                  <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4" />
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
              <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate DataType="vm:MacroCoTriggerViewModel">
                  <Button Classes="toggleSegment"
                          Classes.selected="{Binding IsOn}"
                          ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Caption}"
                          Command="{Binding $parent[views:MacroInspectorPanelView].((vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel)DataContext).ToggleCoTriggerCommand}"
                          CommandParameter="{Binding}">
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Side}"
                                 Classes="monoValueSmall"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding HasSide}"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Symbol}"
                                 Classes="keySymbol"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding HasMark}"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Caption}"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding !HasMark}"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    </StackPanel>
                  </Button>
                </DataTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            </ItemsControl>

            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                       Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.TriggerJoin}"
                       Classes="meta muted"
                       IsVisible="{Binding HasCoTriggers}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <!-- Mono: this is literally the token on the trigger side of the macro line (06 §3). -->
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="2"
                       Text="{Binding TriggerTokenText}"
                       Classes="monoValue"
                       TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          </Grid>

          <!-- The two roles are bound EXCLUSIVELY rather than layered, so which one wins is a fact
               about the selector and never about the order the stylesheet happens to declare them
               in (docs/app/design-system.md, contract 7). -->
          <TextBlock Text="{Binding TriggerStatus}"
                     Classes="meta"
                     Classes.muted="{Binding !IsTriggerAdvisory}"
                     Classes.statusWarning="{Binding IsTriggerAdvisory}"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap" />
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <!-- ===== The name ======================================================================
           "The macro is picked by name from a dropdown — Sign-off block ▾ — with the reuse note
           'Named, so it can be picked for another key from this same dropdown. Also on [f7] · Fn.'"

           PICKING IS ASSIGNING; RENAMING IS THE MACROS TAB'S. 2i splits the two deliberately, so
           there is no name field here — only the list of what the profile already holds. -->
      <Border Classes="inspectorSection">
        <StackPanel Spacing="6">
          <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.NameLabel}" Classes="sectionLabel" />

          <ComboBox ItemsSource="{Binding NameOptions}"
                    SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedName, Mode=TwoWay}"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Stretch">
            <ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
              <DataTemplate DataType="vm:MacroNameOptionViewModel">
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Caption}" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
              </DataTemplate>
            </ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
          </ComboBox>

          <!-- Said only once the macro really carries a name, because that is exactly what makes
               it pickable for a second key. -->
          <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.ReuseNote}"
                     Classes="body muted"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     IsVisible="{Binding IsNamed}" />

          <!-- Where else this same macro fires from, built from the library entry's other sites. -->
          <TextBlock Text="{Binding AlsoOnText}"
                     Classes="meta muted"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     IsVisible="{Binding HasAlsoOnText}" />
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <!-- ===== The steps =====================================================================
           Headed `Steps`, with `drag ⠿ · ⌥↑↓` as the reorder affordance and `● Record` beside it. -->
      <Border Classes="inspectorSection">
        <StackPanel Spacing="8">
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6">
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                       Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.SectionTitle}"
                       Classes="cardTitle"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <!-- `drag ⠿ · ⌥↑↓` — the word, the grip drawn as geometry, and the keyboard spelling
                 for this platform.

                 BOTH marks are DRAWN, not typed: U+283F (the grip) and U+2325 (Option) are in
                 neither embedded IBM Plex family, so either character would be tofu. The ⌥ follows
                 the layer chips exactly (issue #109) — `IconOption` beside the text, hidden off
                 macOS where the text itself reads `Alt+`. An Icon paints from Stroke and cannot
                 inherit Foreground, which is what `Icon.kbdMark` is for. -->
            <StackPanel Grid.Column="1"
                        Orientation="Horizontal"
                        Spacing="3"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.ReorderHintPrefix}" Classes="meta muted" />
              <controls:Icon Classes="dragHandle" Data="{DynamicResource IconDragHandle}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <controls:Icon Classes="kbdMark"
                             Data="{DynamicResource IconOption}"
                             IsVisible="{Binding Steps.ShowsOptionMark}"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Steps.ReorderShortcut}" Classes="meta muted" />
            </StackPanel>

            <Button Grid.Column="2"
                    Classes="recordAction"
                    Command="{Binding RecordCommand}"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                <Ellipse Classes="recordDot" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <!-- The 1.4s pulse (DurationRecordingPulse), reached by the class the listening cap
                     and the Tap & hold panel already share. Bound, so it runs only while this panel
                     is the one capturing. -->
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding RecordCommandCaption}"
                           Classes.recording="{Binding IsRecording}"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              </StackPanel>
            </Button>
          </Grid>

          <!-- The live banner, naming the step the next keystroke lands in. Amber: it is the app
               telling the user their keyboard has been taken over, not an error. -->
          <Border Background="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBrush}"
                  BorderBrush="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBorderBrush}"
                  BorderThickness="1"
                  CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}"
                  Padding="9,7"
                  IsVisible="{Binding IsRecording}">
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding RecordingBanner}" Classes="body statusWarning" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
          </Border>

          <!-- What the capture does with what it hears, verbatim from 2i. -->
          <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.CaptureRule}"
                     Classes="meta muted"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap" />

          <!-- ...and the one thing it cannot do, said in the same breath (issue #128). A chord the
               window system keeps is consumed ABOVE the application and is never delivered here at
               all, so this is a fact about the platform and not a defect the panel can fix; the
               sentence therefore points at the composer below rather than apologising. Its own
               line, because the sentence above it is the mock's wording verbatim and pinned. -->
          <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.OsReservedNote}"
                     Classes="meta muted"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap" />

          <!-- The rows. An ItemsControl and NOT a ListBox, deliberately: the editor's grammar
               leaves the arrows to any SelectingItemsControl in the focused ancestry, so a
               selecting list here would swallow the very ⌥↑↓ the header advertises. Selection is
               the view model's own. -->
          <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Steps.Items}">
            <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
              <DataTemplate DataType="vm:MacroInspectorStepViewModel">
                <!-- The row is a Grid rather than one big Button, and the delete and delay actions
                     sit BESIDE the selecting button rather than inside it. Two reasons, and both
                     have bitten this codebase: a button nested in a button fires both commands on
                     one click, and a `$parent[...]` binding written inside a themed button's
                     content no longer reaches past the presenter (docs/app/design-system.md,
                     "The control-theme layer"). Every ancestor binding here is therefore anchored
                     on this view, whose DataContext is the panel. -->
                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto,Auto"
                      ColumnSpacing="4"
                      Background="Transparent"
                      Classes.dragSource="{Binding IsDragSource}">
                  <controls:Icon Grid.Column="0"
                                 Classes="dragHandle"
                                 Data="{DynamicResource IconDragHandle}"
                                 ToolTip.Tip="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.ReorderHandleHint}"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />

                  <!-- The row's face is the shared `MacroStepCaption` template, because the drag
                       ghost above the list has to be the same six runs in the same two faces and a
                       second copy of them would drift from this one. -->
                  <Button Grid.Column="1"
                          Classes="macroStepRow"
                          Classes.selected="{Binding IsSelected}"
                          Command="{Binding $parent[views:MacroInspectorPanelView].((vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel)DataContext).Steps.SelectStepCommand}"
                          CommandParameter="{Binding}"
                          Content="{Binding}"
                          ContentTemplate="{StaticResource MacroStepCaption}" />

                  <!-- The per-step delay, edited in place — this is where §11.3's dialog went. A
                       row with none offers `delay…`; a row with one shows it in mono, because
                       `80 ms` is a `{d080}` token in the macro.

                       GHOST, NOT LINK. It was a `link` until a captured frame showed what that
                       costs: one accent-coloured affordance per row, three of them stacked beside a
                       red Record, against a design law that gives the accent exactly two meanings
                       (selection/focus, and "you changed this"). A per-row affordance is neither.
                       Chromeless and muted, like the `×` beside it. -->
                  <Button Grid.Column="2"
                          Classes="ghost"
                          Command="{Binding $parent[views:MacroInspectorPanelView].((vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel)DataContext).Steps.EditDelayCommand}"
                          CommandParameter="{Binding}">
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="3">
                      <TextBlock Text="·" Classes="meta muted" IsVisible="{Binding HasDelay}" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding DelayText}" Classes="monoValueSmall" IsVisible="{Binding HasDelay}" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.AddDelayCaption}"
                                 Classes="meta muted"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding !HasDelay}" />
                    </StackPanel>
                  </Button>

                  <Button Grid.Column="3"
                          Classes="ghost"
                          Content="×"
                          ToolTip.Tip="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.RemoveStepHint}"
                          Command="{Binding $parent[views:MacroInspectorPanelView].((vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel)DataContext).Steps.RemoveStepCommand}"
                          CommandParameter="{Binding}" />

                  <!-- WHERE THE DRAG WOULD LAND. A reorder with no feedback reads as broken even
                       once it works, so the row under a carried step wears a ring while the
                       pointer is over it.

                       A RING, NOT A FILL: the selected row already takes `AccentSelectionFill`, and
                       a drop target painted in it would be indistinguishable from the selection it
                       is about to displace. Last child and `IsHitTestVisible=False`, so it draws
                       over the row button's own face without ever becoming the thing the release
                       hit-tests to. -->
                  <Border Grid.Column="0"
                          Grid.ColumnSpan="4"
                          BorderBrush="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}"
                          BorderThickness="1"
                          CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}"
                          IsHitTestVisible="False"
                          IsVisible="{Binding IsDropTarget}" />
                </Grid>
              </DataTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
          </ItemsControl>

          <!-- The trailing row of 2i's list. It runs the same action as `● Record` — a step's
               content is a keystroke, and recording one is how it is made — and the number is the
               step the banner will name. -->
          <Button Classes="macroStepRow" Command="{Binding InsertStepCommand}">
            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6" VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Steps.NextStepNumberText}" Classes="monoValueSmall muted" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <controls:Icon Classes="insertMark" Data="{DynamicResource IconInsert}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.InsertStepCaption}"
                         Classes="meta"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Button>

          <!-- ===== §11.3, with its window taken off ==========================================
               The random radio, the custom 1-999 field with its clamped steppers, and §11.3's own
               validation sentence — over the row the delay belongs to, rather than in a modal over
               a board the user cannot see any more. -->
          <Border Classes="macroDelayEditor" IsVisible="{Binding Steps.IsDelayEditorOpen}">
            <StackPanel Spacing="6">
              <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.DelaySectionTitle}" Classes="sectionLabel" />

              <!-- The firmware gate answered IN PLACE, exactly as the Tap & hold panel answers
                   its own: a refusal for a surface already on screen is an interruption with
                   nothing to interrupt. -->
              <StackPanel Spacing="8" IsVisible="{Binding !Steps.AreDelaysAvailable}">
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Steps.DelayUnavailableReason}"
                           Classes="body statusWarning"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                <Button Classes="secondary"
                        Content="{Binding Steps.UpdateFirmwareCaption}"
                        Command="{Binding Steps.UpdateFirmwareCommand}"
                        IsVisible="{Binding Steps.CanUpdateFirmware}"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Left" />
              </StackPanel>

              <StackPanel Spacing="6" IsVisible="{Binding Steps.AreDelaysAvailable}">
                <!-- The caption is a TextBlock on the type scale rather than a bare string: a
                     RadioButton's own content takes Fluent's default step, which a frame showed
                     reading a size larger than everything around it in the rail. -->
                <RadioButton IsChecked="{Binding Steps.IsRandomDelay, Mode=TwoWay}">
                  <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.RandomDelayCaption}" Classes="body" />
                </RadioButton>

                <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.CustomDelayCaption}" Classes="meta muted" />

                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6">
                  <!-- A real TextBox, and that is correct here: focus landing in one ENDS the
                       recording — the editor's `GotFocus` leg of `StopRecordingOnInteraction`
                       stands the panel down and the banner goes with it — so the digits reach the
                       field and the panel is never left claiming to record while the keyboard
                       belongs to the box (docs/app/keystroke-capture.md, invariant 7: a surface
                       that says it is capturing must never be wrong about it). The service's own
                       auto-suspend on text focus still applies underneath, but it is silent, and a
                       silent suspension is exactly what this panel must not rely on.

                       It is the opposite of an action field, which records a KEYPRESS and must
                       never be focusable. Mono — the count is written into the macro verbatim.

                       The steppers are captioned `+`/`-` rather than `▲`/`▼`: the triangles are
                       not in either embedded family, and an arithmetic sign is not an icon
                       standing in for a mark. -->
                  <TextBox Grid.Column="0"
                           Classes="monoValue"
                           Text="{Binding Steps.CustomDelayMilliseconds, Mode=TwoWay}" />
                  <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4">
                    <Button Content="+" MinWidth="32" Command="{Binding Steps.IncreaseDelayCommand}" />
                    <Button Content="-" MinWidth="32" Command="{Binding Steps.DecreaseDelayCommand}" />
                  </StackPanel>
                </Grid>

                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Steps.DelayError}"
                           Classes="body statusError"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           IsVisible="{Binding Steps.HasDelayError}" />

                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6">
                  <Button Classes="primaryAction"
                          Content="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.ApplyDelayCaption}"
                          Command="{Binding Steps.ApplyDelayCommand}" />
                  <Button Classes="secondary"
                          Content="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.ClearDelayCaption}"
                          Command="{Binding Steps.ClearDelayCommand}" />
                  <Button Classes="secondary"
                          Content="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.CloseDelayCaption}"
                          Command="{Binding Steps.CloseDelayCommand}" />
                </StackPanel>
              </StackPanel>
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <!-- What the panel refused, and why. Amber: a budget or a full slot strip is a fact about
               the profile, not a failure. -->
          <TextBlock Text="{Binding Message}"
                     Classes="body statusWarning"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     IsVisible="{Binding HasMessage}" />
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <!-- ===== The chord composer (issue #128) ===============================================
           THE ANSWER TO A CHORD THAT CANNOT BE PRESSED. `Ctrl+1` switches desktop on macOS and a
           hotkey utility can claim any other combination; such a chord is eaten by the window
           system above this application and is never delivered to it, so no amount of capture work
           reaches it (docs/app/keystroke-capture.md). Rather than promise a fix that would do
           nothing, the panel makes pressing unnecessary: tick the modifiers, pick the key, insert.

           IT IS THE SAME PICKER, over the same catalog, that the rail's Remap panel and §11.6's
           insertion modal host — and the same session `Recent` history behind them — so this is a
           fourth call site of one control and not a second key search.

           CLOSED AT REST. The rail is 300 px wide and the picker alone is most of a screenful; the
           composer is the answer to an occasional problem, not the panel's main affordance, so it
           is disclosed rather than always drawn. -->
      <Border Classes="inspectorSection">
        <StackPanel Spacing="8">
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6">
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                       Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.ComposerLabel}"
                       Classes="sectionLabel"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <Button Grid.Column="1"
                    Classes="secondary"
                    Content="{Binding ComposerToggleCaption}"
                    Command="{Binding ToggleComposerCommand}"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          </Grid>

          <StackPanel Spacing="8" IsVisible="{Binding IsComposerOpen}">
            <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.ComposerHint}"
                       Classes="meta muted"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap" />

            <!-- THE EIGHT MODIFIERS ARE MARKS, exactly as the co-trigger strip below draws its six
                 — `⇧ R⇧ ⌃ R⌃ ⌥ R⌥ ⌘ R⌘`, left unmarked and only the right side spelled, with the
                 words in the tooltip because a mark alone is not accessible text. Neither run
                 carries a colour: `ToggleSegment`'s `.selected` face sets Foreground, a TextBlock
                 inherits it, and a `muted` here would break the on-accent label. -->
            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding ChordModifiers}">
              <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                  <WrapPanel />
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
              <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate DataType="vm:MacroChordModifier">
                  <Button Classes="toggleSegment"
                          Classes.selected="{Binding IsOn}"
                          Margin="0,0,4,4"
                          ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Description}"
                          Command="{Binding $parent[views:MacroInspectorPanelView].((vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel)DataContext).ToggleChordModifierCommand}"
                          CommandParameter="{Binding}">
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Side}"
                                 Classes="monoValueSmall"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding HasSide}"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Symbol}"
                                 Classes="keySymbol"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    </StackPanel>
                  </Button>
                </DataTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            </ItemsControl>

            <!-- The chord as the step row will draw it, beside the button that writes it. It is
                 ABOVE the picker deliberately: the result list is capped at 300 px and the rail
                 scrolls, so an Insert button placed after it would be off-screen for the whole of
                 the pick. The preview is read back off a real Keystroke, so it can never promise a
                 modifier the model drops (05 §5.1 attaches none to a modifier key). -->
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6">
              <StackPanel Grid.Column="0"
                          Orientation="Horizontal"
                          Spacing="5"
                          VerticalAlignment="Center"
                          IsVisible="{Binding HasComposedKey}">
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding ComposedTokenText}"
                           Classes="monoValue"
                           Foreground="{DynamicResource MacroStepKeyBrush}"
                           TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding ComposedMarks}"
                              ItemTemplate="{StaticResource MacroModifierMarkRun}"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center">
                  <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                    <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="3" />
                    </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                </ItemsControl>
              </StackPanel>

              <Button Grid.Column="1"
                      Classes="primaryAction"
                      Content="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.InsertChordCaption}"
                      Command="{Binding InsertChordCommand}"
                      VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Grid>

            <!-- Resolved by ViewLocator from the panel's own TokenPickerViewModel, exactly as the
                 Remap panel hosts its own. `↵` on a highlighted row inserts the chord too — the
                 picker raises `Chosen` and the panel decides what taking a row means. -->
            <ContentControl Content="{Binding ChordPicker}" />
          </StackPanel>
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <!-- ===== The footer meters =============================================================
           2i's three readings, in its order: `Playback speed 3 / 5`, `this macro 128 / 500`,
           `layout keystrokes 5 140 / 7 200`. Over budget goes AMBER and still saves — the layout
           is written as it stands and the board truncates. -->
      <Border Classes="inspectorSection">
        <StackPanel Spacing="8">
          <StackPanel Spacing="4">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" IsVisible="{Binding HasSpeed}">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding SpeedMeter.Label}" Classes="body" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                         Text="{Binding SpeedMeter.Caption}"
                         Classes="monoValue"
                         Classes.statusWarning="{Binding SpeedMeter.IsOverBudget}"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Grid>

            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding MacroLengthMeter.Label}" Classes="body" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                         Text="{Binding MacroLengthMeter.Caption}"
                         Classes="monoValue"
                         Classes.statusWarning="{Binding MacroLengthMeter.IsOverBudget}"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Grid>

            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding LayoutKeystrokeMeter.Label}" Classes="body" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                         Text="{Binding LayoutKeystrokeMeter.Caption}"
                         Classes="monoValue"
                         Classes.statusWarning="{Binding LayoutKeystrokeMeter.IsOverBudget}"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Grid>
          </StackPanel>

          <!-- Speed itself, beside the reading that counts it. -->
          <Slider Minimum="{Binding SpeedMinimum}"
                  Maximum="{Binding SpeedMaximum}"
                  Value="{Binding Speed, Mode=TwoWay}"
                  TickFrequency="1"
                  IsSnapToTickEnabled="True"
                  IsVisible="{Binding HasSpeed}" />

          <!-- REPEAT STAYS. Mockup 2i draws only speed; repeat is supported by the devices this app
               edits today and there is nowhere else left to set it once the Macros tab becomes a
               library, so dropping it would be dropping a feature silently. Recorded as a deviation
               in docs/app/design-system.md. THE CO-TRIGGERS LEFT THIS BLOCK with issue #137: they
               are the Trigger strip in the header now, where they sit beside the trigger token they
               qualify and the collision they cause.

               Absent, never disabled, where the file does not keep it: the Advantage2 has a repeat
               range in the model but its serializer writes no {xN} token at all. -->
          <StackPanel Spacing="4" IsVisible="{Binding HasRepeat}">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                         Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.RepeatLabel}"
                         Classes="body"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Repeat}" Classes="monoValue" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Grid>
            <Slider Minimum="{Binding RepeatMinimum}"
                    Maximum="{Binding RepeatMaximum}"
                    Value="{Binding Repeat, Mode=TwoWay}"
                    TickFrequency="1"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" />
          </StackPanel>

        </StackPanel>
      </Border>
    </StackPanel>
    </StackPanel>

    <!-- ===== The carried step ================================================================
         WHAT IS BEING DRAGGED, not only where it would land (issue #128). The drop ring alone left
         the gesture's subject invisible — the list did not move until the pointer came up, which is
         exactly the "the view is only updated after the movement" the user reported. A copy of the
         row now rides the pointer from the 4 px threshold until the gesture ends.

         `IsHitTestVisible="False"` IS MANDATORY, on the layer and on the ghost alike. The drop row
         is resolved by hit-testing the release position against the live tree, so a ghost that
         could be hit would sit under the pointer and answer every one of those tests itself — the
         drag would land on nothing, every time.

         Its content is the row's own `MacroStepCaption`, and its position is written straight from
         `OnPointerMovedOverStep`; a Canvas measures to nothing, so this costs the panel above it no
         height at all. -->
    <Canvas x:Name="DragLayer" IsHitTestVisible="False">
      <Border x:Name="DragGhost"
              Classes="macroStepGhost"
              IsHitTestVisible="False"
              IsVisible="False">
        <ContentControl x:Name="DragGhostContent" ContentTemplate="{StaticResource MacroStepCaption}" />
      </Border>
    </Canvas>
  </Grid>
</UserControl>
